<h2>The accusation and the answer</h2>
<p>The petitioners headed Tirhut Lokseva Sansthan, the society operating a Kasturba Gandhi Balika Vidyalaya — a residential school scheme for girls. The FIR alleged misappropriation of Rs. 6,59,097 of government money, with the familiar cocktail of Sections 406, 409 and 420 IPC. The defence answer was structural rather than rhetorical: every transaction required the joint signatures of the school's Secretary and Warden; the disputed amount was never debited to or withdrawn by the petitioners; it had been credited to the Income Tax Department against tax liabilities; and an appeal under the Income Tax Act had been filed to bring the money back to the school's account.</p>
<p>The State opposed bail on the strength of the second petitioner's criminal antecedents. The court granted protection to both.</p>

<h2>The factors the court assembled</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Vintage of the FIR.</strong> A 2019 case, with the petitioners asserting recent knowledge and a report showing no coercive process issued — years of investigative quiet undercut any claimed necessity of arrest.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The money trail.</strong> The amount was not withdrawn or credited to the petitioners' accounts; it sat with the Income Tax Department, with recovery steps already taken. The alleged gain, in short, was nowhere to be found with the accused.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>A documentary case.</strong> Account books, bank records and sanction papers decide such prosecutions; there was nothing custodial interrogation could extract that the documents would not show.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>The persons before the court.</strong> Senior citizens of 62 and 67, rooted and with no realistic flight risk.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">
<p>The conditions reflect the modern template: modest bonds with two sureties, one surety from the family — an assurance of traceability — and cooperation with the investigation as a continuing obligation, enforceable by cancellation if breached.</p>
</div>

<h2>Economic offences and the arrest question</h2>
<p>Courts repeat that economic offences stand on a different footing, and so they do where deep-rooted conspiracies and large-scale diversion of public funds appear. But the label "economic offence" does not decide bail by itself. The Supreme Court's arrest jurisprudence — from Joginder Kumar and Arnesh Kumar to the line of cases discouraging mechanical custody in chargesheet-ready, document-driven prosecutions — asks a more particular question: what will arrest accomplish that the record cannot? Where the answer is nothing, anticipatory bail with cooperation conditions reconciles the investigation's needs with personal liberty.</p>
<p>The order also illustrates how a well-prepared defence reframes a misappropriation case at the threshold. Tracing the funds — not asserting innocence, but showing where the money actually went — converts a trust-violation narrative into an accounting dispute, and accounting disputes rarely justify custody.</p>

<h2>Practical pointers</h2>
<div class="check">
<p>In funds-misappropriation FIRs, reconstruct the money trail first: bank statements, counterfoils, departmental correspondence. The destination of the money is the case.</p>
<p>Obtain the case diary position on coercive steps; long investigative dormancy is a bail argument in itself.</p>
<p>Where statutory appeals or departmental remedies concerning the same funds are pending, place them on record — parallel civil machinery signals a non-custodial dispute.</p>
<p>Expect surety conditions anchored in the family, and treat cooperation directions as strict obligations; a single default can undo the protection.</p>
</div>
